.\" /usr/bin/textutil.
.TH TEXTUTIL 1 "September 27, 2026" "LibreDarwin"
.SH NAME
\fBtextutil\fR \- convert text between formats
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B textutil
[\fB\-help\fR]
[\fIcommand_option\fR] [\fIother_options\fR] \fIfile\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B textutil
converts a file between plain text, RTF, RTFD and HTML, and reports what a
file is with
.BR -info .
It reads UTF-8 plain text by default, keeping a byte order mark out of the
text, and writes UTF-8 unless
.B \-encoding
says otherwise.
.PP
A file is taken as what its name says it is, and
.B \-format
overrides that. A name that is not one of the formats below is read as plain
text, and the contents are then used to tell the rich formats apart: a file
that begins with
.B {\\rtf1
is RTF, a folder holding a
.B TXT.rtf
is an RTFD bundle, and a file that begins with a tag or a
.BR <!DOCTYPE >
is HTML.
.PP
With no command option,
.B \-help
is the default and prints the usage message.
.SH COMMANDS
Exactly one command may be given.
.TP
.B \-help
Print the usage message and exit.
.TP
.B \-info
For each input file, print its name, the format it was read as, its size in
bytes, its length in UTF-16 code units, its metadata, and a preview of its
first line. The preview is cut to 30 code units and ends in
.B ...
when anything was left out. A line separator ends the preview as a newline
does, and what is behind one is not shown. An empty file has no preview.
.TP
.BI \-convert " fmt"
Read each input file and write it in
.IR fmt ,
which is one of
.BR txt ,
.BR rtf ,
.BR rtfd ,
.BR html ,
.BR doc ,
.BR docx ,
.BR odt ,
.B wordml
or
.BR webarchive .
.TP
.BI \-cat " fmt"
Read every input file and write them as one file in
.IR fmt .
Only the first input file's name is used for the output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-extension " ext"
Use
.I ext
in place of the input file's extension when naming an output file.
.TP
.BI \-output " path"
Write the first output file to
.IR path .
.TP
.B \-stdin
Read from standard input instead of from the file list. Any file named on the
command line is then an error, because the two cannot both be the input.
.TP
.B \-stdout
Write the first output file to standard output instead of to a file.
.TP
.BI \-encoding " name"
Encode plain text and HTML output with
.IR name ,
which is an IANA name such as
.B UTF-8
or
.BR ISO-Latin-1 ,
or an
.B NSStringEncoding
number.
.B UTF-8
is the default. The option has no effect on RTF, RTFD or the office
containers.
.TP
.BI \-inputencoding " name"
Read plain text input as
.IR name .
By default the encoding is taken from a byte order mark, and failing that
assumed to be UTF-8.
.TP
.BI \-format " fmt"
Read every input file as
.I fmt
rather than deciding from its name and contents. This is how a plain text file
is read as something richer.
.TP
.BI \-font " font"
The font to use when converting plain text to rich text.
.TP
.BI \-fontsize " size"
The font size to use when converting plain text to rich text.
.TP
.B \-\-
Every argument after this one is a file name, even one that begins with a
hyphen.
.TP
.B \-noload
Do not load the resources an HTML file refers to.
.TP
.B \-nostore
Do not write out the resources an HTML file refers to.
.TP
.BI \-baseurl " url"
The base URL for the resources an HTML file refers to.
.TP
.BI \-timeout " t"
How many seconds to wait for an HTML resource to load.
.TP
.BI \-textsizemultiplier " x"
Multiply the font sizes in an HTML file by
.IR x .
.TP
.BI \-excludedelements " (tag1, tag2, ...)"
Leave the named HTML elements out of HTML output.
.TP
.BI \-prefixspaces " n"
Indent nested HTML output by
.I n
spaces.
.TP
.B \-strip
Leave the input's metadata out of the output.
.TP
.BI \-title " val"
The title to record in the output.
.TP
.BI \-author " val"
The author to record in the output.
.TP
.BI \-subject " val"
The subject to record in the output.
.TP
.BI \-keywords " (val1, val2, ...)"
The keywords to record in the output, as a comma separated list.
.TP
.BI \-comment " val"
The comment to record in the output.
.TP
.BI \-editor " val"
The last editor to record in the output.
.TP
.BI \-company " val"
The company to record in the output.
.TP
.BI \-creationtime " yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ssZ"
The creation time to record in the output, in UTC.
.TP
.BI \-modificationtime " yyyy-mm-ddThh:mm:ssZ"
The modification time to record in the output, in UTC.
.SH OUTPUT
The first output file is named after the first input file, with the extension
the command gives it. Each further input file takes the next available name,
and
.B \-output
overrides the first. A destination that cannot be written to is reported and
stops the run before anything is written.
.PP
An RTFD bundle is a folder holding the RTF as
.BR TXT.rtf .
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, and also when a file cannot be read, which is reported on
standard error instead.
.B -cat
differs from the other commands here: it returns 1 on a read failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
Report what a file is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
textutil \-info notes.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Make an RTF bundle of two plain text files, joined:
.PP
.RS
.nf
textutil \-cat rtf \-output report.rtfd a.txt b.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take the metadata off a file and write the text back out:
.PP
.RS
.nf
textutil \-strip \-convert txt \-output clean.txt draft.rtf
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iconv (1),
.BR plutil (1),
.BR sed (1)
.SH NOTES
.RB ` NOTES.md '
in the source tree records what the reference implementation does in the
cases where its behaviour is surprising, and where this port deliberately
differs from it.
